    "slug": "get-featured-in-forbes",
    "h1": "How to Get Featured in Forbes",
    "title": "How to Get Featured in Forbes | DNA PR",
    "description": "Forbes is the most recognised business brand in the world. The real routes in, what each involves, and how to build a story worth publishing.",
    "body": "<p>Forbes is the name almost everyone names first. It is also the hardest to get, and the most misunderstood.</p>\n<p>Here is how it actually works.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-featured-in-forbes-actually-means\">What \"featured in Forbes\" actually means</h2>\n<p>Four different things get called a Forbes feature, and they are not the same product.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Staff or contributor editorial.</strong> A Forbes writer decides you are worth covering. Free, and entirely their call.</li><li><strong>Forbes Councils.</strong> A paid membership that lets you publish your own bylined posts. Application-based, with published revenue requirements — reported at roughly $2,500–$2,700 a year plus a one-time initiation fee.</li><li><strong>Forbes BrandVoice.</strong> Sponsored content produced for a brand, carrying a paid-content label. Reported from around $50,000.</li><li><strong>Agency-assisted pitching.</strong> An agency develops your story and pitches it to the right writer. You pay for the work, not the outcome.</li></ul>\n<p>Knowing which one you are being offered is the single most useful thing on this page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-forbes-is-worth-pursuing-and-when-it-is-not\">Why Forbes is worth pursuing — and when it is not</h2>\n<p>A Forbes link is understood by everyone. No explanation needed, no context required. For some founders that is worth a great deal.</p>\n<p>But it is a poor first move. Editorial coverage goes to people who already have a public record — a track record of coverage, data worth citing, a story a writer can verify. Chasing Forbes with no press history is the most common way founders waste a year.</p>\n<p>Build the record first. Forbes gets easier from there.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-makes-a-story-forbes-will-run\">What makes a story Forbes will run</h2>\n<p>Writers are not looking for successful people. They are looking for stories.</p>\n<p>That means a number nobody else has, a trend you can evidence, a genuinely contrarian view, or a moment that makes your story timely. \"Founder builds profitable company\" is not a story. \"Here is what our data shows about why this market is about to break\" is.</p>\n<p>If you cannot say what the reader learns, it is not ready to pitch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-get-featured\">How to get featured</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pitch it.</strong> Find the writers who cover your space, read what they actually publish, and send something short and specific. Free, slow, and low odds — but it is the real thing.</li><li><strong>Use a professional feature service.</strong> A team builds the story, identifies the right targets and pitches on your behalf. Faster and far more likely to land, because the work is done properly.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-expect-from-the-process\">What to expect from the process</h2>\n<p>We develop the angle with you, write to editorial standard, and pitch the writers who cover your space. You approve the story before anything goes out.</p>\n<p>One honest note: no agency controls Forbes editorial. We pitch, we position, and we are good at it — but anyone guaranteeing you a Forbes placement is selling something they cannot deliver. Be careful with that promise wherever you hear it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2>\n<p>Digital Networking Agency builds the story and the press record that makes coverage like this possible — starting with publications where we place clients consistently, and building toward the bigger names.</p>\n<p>If Forbes is the goal, the fastest route is rarely to start there.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can you pay to be featured in Forbes?</h3>\n<p>You can pay for Forbes Councils membership or BrandVoice sponsored content, both of which are official Forbes products. You cannot pay for staff editorial coverage — that is an editorial decision.</p>\n<h3>How hard is it to get editorial coverage?</h3>\n<p>Hard. Writers receive far more pitches than they can run, and most go unanswered. A specific story with real evidence behind it dramatically improves the odds.</p>\n<h3>Should Forbes be my first placement?</h3>\n<p>Usually not. Coverage tends to follow coverage, so building a genuine press record first makes the bigger names considerably more achievable.</p>",
