    "slug": "how-to-get-featured-in-entrepreneur",
    "h1": "How to Get Featured in Entrepreneur Magazine",
    "title": "How to Get Featured in Entrepreneur Magazine | DNA PR",
    "description": "The real routes into Entrepreneur — staff editorial, the Leadership Network, and agency pitching — and how to build a story worth publishing.",
    "body": "<p>Entrepreneur is one of the few business titles founders name without thinking. It has been covering small business and startups since 1977, and a feature there still reads as a genuine credential.</p>\n<p>Here is how it actually works.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-featured-in-entrepreneur-actually-means\">What \"featured in Entrepreneur\" actually means</h2>\n<p>Several different things get called an Entrepreneur feature, and they are not the same product.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Staff editorial.</strong> An Entrepreneur writer or editor decides your story is worth covering. Free, and entirely their decision.</li><li><strong>Entrepreneur Leadership Network (ELN).</strong> A paid membership programme that lets accepted members publish bylined articles on entrepreneur.com. Application-based, with tiered annual pricing.</li><li><strong>Sponsored and partner content.</strong> Paid placements produced with or for a brand, carrying a paid-content label.</li><li><strong>Agency-assisted pitching.</strong> An agency develops your story and pitches it to the right editor or writer. You pay for the work, not a guaranteed outcome.</li></ul>\n<p>Working out which of these you are being offered is the most useful thing on this page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-entrepreneur-is-worth-pursuing\">Why Entrepreneur is worth pursuing</h2>\n<p>Entrepreneur sits in a useful place. It is instantly recognised, but its audience is specifically founders, operators and small-business owners — so a feature there reaches people who might actually buy from you, not just people who are impressed by the logo.</p>\n<p>For a founder selling to other founders, that focus is often worth more than a broader mainstream name.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-makes-a-story-entrepreneur-will-run\">What makes a story Entrepreneur will run</h2>\n<p>Entrepreneur publishes practical, useful material. Its readers are building something, and the pieces that land help them do it.</p>\n<p>That means a lesson learned the hard way, a number from inside your business nobody else can give, a counterintuitive take on how your industry works, or a genuine tactical playbook. \"Founder launches company\" is not a story. \"What our first 100 customers taught us about pricing\" is.</p>\n<p>If you cannot say what the reader walks away able to do, it is not ready to pitch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-get-featured\">How to get featured</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pitch it yourself.</strong> Read what individual writers actually publish, then send something short, specific and relevant to their beat. Free, slow, and low odds — but it is the real thing.</li><li><strong>Apply to the Leadership Network.</strong> If you qualify and want a bylined column, this is an official route with a published application process.</li><li><strong>Use a professional feature service.</strong> A team builds the story, identifies the right targets and pitches on your behalf.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-expect-from-the-process\">What to expect from the process</h2>\n<p>We develop the angle with you, write it to editorial standard, and pitch it where it fits. You approve every word before anything is submitted.</p>\n<p>One honest note: no agency controls Entrepreneur's editorial decisions. We pitch and position, and we are good at it — but anyone guaranteeing you an Entrepreneur placement is selling something they cannot deliver. Be careful with that promise wherever you hear it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2>\n<p>Digital Networking Agency builds the story and the press record that makes coverage like this possible — starting with publications where we place clients consistently, then building toward the bigger names.</p>\n<p>Coverage follows coverage. If Entrepreneur is the goal, starting there is rarely the fastest route.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can you pay to be featured in Entrepreneur?</h3>\n<p>You can pay for Entrepreneur Leadership Network membership or sponsored content, both of which are official Entrepreneur products. You cannot pay for staff editorial coverage — that is an editorial decision.</p>\n<h3>Is the Entrepreneur Leadership Network worth it?</h3>\n<p>It depends what you want. It gives you a bylined presence on a recognised domain, which some founders value highly. It is not the same as being written about by a staff journalist, and you should be clear which one you are buying.</p>\n<h3>Should Entrepreneur be my first placement?</h3>\n<p>Usually not. Editorial coverage tends to go to people with an existing public record, so building that first makes the bigger names considerably more achievable.</p>",
    "toc": [
      { "id": "what-featured-in-entrepreneur-actually-means", "text": "What \"featured in Entrepreneur\" actually means" },
      { "id": "why-entrepreneur-is-worth-pursuing", "text": "Why Entrepreneur is worth pursuing" },
      { "id": "what-makes-a-story-entrepreneur-will-run", "text": "What makes a story Entrepreneur will run" },
      { "id": "how-to-get-featured", "text": "How to get featured" },
      { "id": "what-to-expect-from-the-process", "text": "What to expect from the process" },
      { "id": "getting-started", "text": "Getting started" },
      { "id": "frequently-asked-questions", "text": "Frequently asked questions" }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "\"Featured in Entrepreneur\" covers several different products — know which one you are buying.",
      "Its audience is founders and operators, so the coverage reaches actual buyers.",
      "Entrepreneur publishes practical lessons, not company announcements."
    ]
